Subject: [PATCH] fix(agent_loop): use monotonic clock for elapsed time

Problem
-------
On devices without an RTC the system clock starts at 1970. The TLS
layer corrects it during the first handshake ("Clock too old, forcing
to 2026"). When that correction lands between the two timestamps of an
LLM call, calc_elapsed_ms() reports roughly 31 days. The watchdog
(AGENT_LLM_TIMEOUT_SEC = 60) then treats the call as a timeout and
discards a response that had already arrived successfully
("Response: 284 bytes text, finish=end_turn").

Root cause
----------
gettimeofday() reads CLOCK_REALTIME, which is subject to wall-clock
adjustment. calc_elapsed_ms() already guards against the clock moving
backwards but not against it jumping forward, which is exactly what
happens on RTC-less hardware.

Fix
---
Add mono_now(), which reads CLOCK_MONOTONIC and fills a struct
timeval, and use it at all ten elapsed-time measurement points in
agent_loop.c. Falls back to gettimeofday() if the monotonic clock is
unavailable. calc_elapsed_ms() and every call site keep their
existing signature.

Reminder scheduling (time(NULL), lines 610/659) intentionally keeps
the wall clock, as it needs the actual date.

Verified on ESP32-P4 / openvela, no RTC configured.
Additionally: do not cache timeout or degraded placeholders.

The cache store only checked that final_text was non-NULL. On a
failed call final_text holds LLM_TIMEOUT_MSG, so the error text was
stored as the answer for that prompt. Every later identical prompt
then returned the error straight from cache ("Cache hit, skipping
LLM call") without reaching the network: one transient failure
poisons the prompt permanently.

A forced summary after hitting the tool iteration limit is likewise
a degraded result (the code already marks it AGENT_TRACE_TIMEOUT)
and is now excluded from the cache as well.

